window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-06-1", "source": "2023年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"Today there is a growing awareness that mental well-being needs to be given as much attention as physical health.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Today there is a growing awareness that mental well-being needs to be given as much attention as physical\nhealth. This view deserves our serious attention, especially when modern life keeps us under constant pressure\nand quiet mental troubles often go unnoticed.\nTo begin with, ignoring our inner state can cost us dearly. Long-term stress and anxiety lead to sleeplessness\nand poor concentration, which in turn damage both our bodies and our performance. Take my own experience\nas an example: before an important exam, I was so anxious that I could not sleep for nights and ended up doing\nfar worse than usual. Only after I talked to my friends and kept a regular routine did my mind settle down and\nmy results recover. This taught me that the mind clearly deserves the same care we give the body.\nIn conclusion, we should value our minds as much as our bodies. Only when individuals pay attention to their\nfeelings and society offers more support can we truly live a healthy and balanced life."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国越来越重视终身教育，发展继续教育是构建终身教育体系的有效途径。高校作为人才培养的基地， 拥有先进的教学理念和优越的教学资源， 理应成为继续教育的办学主体。因此，近年来许多高校适应社会需求， 加强与用人单位沟通， 努力探索一条符合中国国情的继续教育发展新路， 以使继续教育在国家发展战略中发挥更大的作用。", "sample": "China attaches increasing importance to lifelong education, and developing continuing education is an effective way to build a lifelong education system. As a base for cultivating talent, universities, possessing advanced teaching philosophies and superior teaching resources, should be the main providers of continuing education. Therefore, in recent years, many universities have been exploring a new path of continuing education that suits China's national conditions by adapting to social needs and strengthening communication with employers, so as to enable continuing education to play a greater role in the national development strategy."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2023-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "You probably haven't taken the time to think of all the work that went into creating the shirt on your back. I mean, how hard it could be to create fabric and  __26__  it into a shirt shape. Don't machines do all that? Well, creating fabric from cotton, which is the most  __27__  clothing material, is actually a process that involves a lot of water, 2,700 liters per shirt to be  __28__  . Take a look at the video below from National Geographic for some more mind-blowing  __29__  about cotton clothing production.\nClean water is  __30__  becoming one of the most sought-after resources in the world. Given how large the  __31__  and cotton industries are, they take up a lot of our fresh water demands across the world, according to The Huffington Post. The video from National Geographic was created to spread  __32__  of how environmentally harmful cotton is. But the situation can be made better. Through better water management and farming practices, water usage in cotton production can be cut down by  __33__  40 percent.\nCalled \"Better Cotton\", this environmentally conscious product will save millions of liters of water a year simply from  __34__  the demands of cotton production. Cotton doesn't have to go, since it is, after all, one of the most useful cash crops across the globe. However, as water supplies  __35__  , farmers and consumers need to be more conscious of the effect that these products have on the environment as a whole.", "matchIntro": "The spoken web", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "We're growing more used to chatting to our computers, phones and smart speakers through voice assistants like Amazon's Alexa, Apple's Siri and Microsoft's Cortana. Blind and partially sighted people have been using text-to-speech converters for decades."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Out of these assistants, Siri is the most well-known. The assistant uses voice inquiries and a natural-language user interface(界面) to answer questions. The software adapts to users' individual language usages, searches, and preferences, with continuing use."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Some think voice could soon take over from typing and clicking as the main way to interact online. But what are the challenges of moving to \"the spoken web\"?"}, {"label": "D", "text": "What use is written online content if you can't read? That is the situation facing illiterate(不识字的) African farmers. They are often denied crucial information the web offers many others. With a literacy rate in some parts of Africa at only 22.6%, farmers are often \"underpaid for their produce because they might be unaware of the prevailing prices,\" says Francis Dittoh, a researcher behind Mr Meteo, a speech-based weather information service."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"The most frequently heard complaint is about rainfall predictions,\" says Mr Dittoh, who lives in Tamale, northern Ghana. \"They tell us the methods their forefathers used to predict the weather don't seem to work as well these days.\" This is down to climate change, he believes. Yet knowing when it's going to rain is vital for farmers wanting to sow seeds, irrigate crops or take their animals out to the fields to feed on grass."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Mr Dittoh says the idea of converting online weather reports into speech came from the farmers themselves, after a workshop in the village of Guabuligah. \"They came up with this,\" he says. Mr Meteo takes the online weather forecast, converts it to a short recording in the appropriate language and makes it available on a basic phone. Farmers ring up to receive the information. The local language Dagbani is spoken by 1.2 million people but is not served by any online translation applications. The service was designed to be cheap and easy to run, says Mr Dittoh. He plans to begin field tests this month, working with Tamale's Savanna Agricultural Research Institute."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The spoken web could also help the one-in-five adults in Europe and the U.S. with poor reading skills. But building the spoken web—web-to-voice and voice-to-web—isn't straightforward. For software to understand that pizza is served at Italian restaurants is easy. To cover multiple domains and to be able to have a conversation with users on every single topic is still a long way off."}, {"label": "H", "text": "So although many computer assistants can answer simple questions about the weather and play music for us, anything resembling a wide-ranging human conversation is decades away. Artificial intelligence just isn't smart enough yet. Even turning your voice into text—automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest problems to solve, as there are as many ways to pronounce things as there are people on the planet."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Siri has often been praised for its ability to interpret our casual language and deliver very specific and accurate results, sometimes even providing additional information. But it is still somewhat restricted, particularly when the language moves away from stiffer commands into more human interactions. In one example, the phrase \"Send a text to Jason, Clint, Sam, and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant\" was interpreted as sending a message to Jason only, containing the text \"Clint Sam and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant\". It has also been noted that Siri lacks a proper editing function, as saying \"Edit message to say: we're at Silver Cloud restaurant and you should come find us\" generates \"Clint Sam and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant to say we're at Silver Cloud restaurant and you should come find us\"."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Using voice interaction feels far more intimate than surfing the net the old-fashioned way. This is intentional as the informal tone of the assistant helps create an emotional attachment. But if something speaks, it must also listen. Our phones are always near us and they are collecting data about us all the time. This has already raised privacy concerns. The American Civil Liberties Union has stated that digital assistants create a threat to privacy from hackers. Some people have other concerns. They worry assistants will one day be used to deliver advertising directly to us."}, {"label": "K", "text": "But digital voices need more personality to make them popular. Robots are not yet witty, Siri is boring. The benefits of using voice instead of tapping fingers obviously depend on the context. Doctors completing online forms about their patients by speech, for example, can dictate 150 words a minute, three times faster than typing on a keyboard. This enables them to spend less time on administration and more time with patients."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Last year, speech recognition company Nuance helped a doctors' surgery in Dukinfield, near Manchester, set up a speech system for the practice's six doctors. Now they can dictate notes on a patient's health condition and treatment and a smart assistant automatically enters the information into the right fields on a web form. Previously, the doctors made voice recordings that were then transcribed by secretaries—a process that was costly and likely to cause delays. The new system means letters to patients now have more detail."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Using voice also makes sense when you're doing other things with your hands. Think about when you're cooking, and you just want to know what the next step in the recipe is. Your hands are covered with oil; you're not going to get on the iPad, so it's a lot more natural to talk. And speech obviously makes sense when you're driving. In the U.S., 29% of drivers admit they surf behind the wheel, according to insurance firm State Farm. This is up from 13% in 2009. No wonder using mobile phones while driving causes more crashes a year than drink driving, says the U.S. National Safety Council."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound(充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities(便利设施). Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.\nThis is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces: bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.\nThere are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.\nThe trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?\nI believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.", "47": "The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound(充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities(便利设施). Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.\nThis is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces: bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.\nThere are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.\nThe trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?\nI believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.", "48": "The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound(充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities(便利设施). Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.\nThis is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces: bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.\nThere are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.\nThe trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?\nI believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.", "49": "The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound(充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities(便利设施). Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.\nThis is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces: bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.\nThere are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.\nThe trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?\nI believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.", "50": "The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound(充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities(便利设施). Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.\nThis is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces: bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.\nThere are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.\nThe trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?\nI believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.", "51": "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.\nOverall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.\nActually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.\nOne major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.\nFew people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.\nIt's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.\nAmbitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional \"success,\" such success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?\nClearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life.", "52": "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.\nOverall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.\nActually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.\nOne major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.\nFew people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.\nIt's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.\nAmbitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional \"success,\" such success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?\nClearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life.", "53": "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.\nOverall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.\nActually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.\nOne major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.\nFew people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.\nIt's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.\nAmbitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional \"success,\" such success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?\nClearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life.", "54": "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.\nOverall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.\nActually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.\nOne major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.\nFew people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.\nIt's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.\nAmbitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional \"success,\" such success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?\nClearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life.", "55": "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.\nOverall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.\nActually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.\nOne major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.\nFew people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.\nIt's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.\nAmbitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional \"success,\" such success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?\nClearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn from the report about the 55-year-old woman?", "options": ["A) She was involved in a conflict with bird lovers.", "B) She was charged with mistreating animals.", "C) She was on bad terms with her neighbors.", "D) She was accused of violating a city law."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She was accused of violating a city law.\n【定位】A woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding\nwild animals.\n一名女子被指控涉嫌违反罗德岛市一项禁止投喂野生动物的市级法规。\n【信号】导语句即答案句。A woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city\nlaw against feeding wild animals. 是整则新闻的第一句导语，集中给出了「主体（女子）\n+ 动作（被指控）+ 内容（违反市法规）」，新闻体裁里「开篇就点核心」是最强的答案\n信号。\n【替换】was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law ↔\nwas accused of violating a city law\ncharge≈accuse 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe was involved in a conflict with bird lovers. 利用录音多次提到的 birds / feeding\nbirds 制造「她与爱鸟者起冲突」的假象，但录音通篇只说她「以为自己在喂鸟」，从未出\n现她和任何 bird lovers 群体的冲突，是典型的「听到 bird 就想选」。\n借用原文：she's being charged for feeding birds\nB) 偷换概念\nShe was charged with mistreating animals. 沿用了录音里 was charged with 的句式结\n构和 animals 这个词，但把违法内容从「违反禁止投喂的市法规」偷换成「虐待动物」\n——录音从未提及任何虐待行为，反而她声称是「出于爱」在喂。\n借用原文：was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding\nwild animals\nC) 过度推断\nShe was on bad terms with her neighbors. 利用录音 The 55-year-old woman's\nneighbors blame her 里出现的 neighbors blame 来诱选；但 blame 是针对此次鼠患的\n具体指责，新闻并未交代他们一贯关系不和，把「具体事件上的指责」升级成「长期关系恶\n劣」属于过度推断。\n借用原文：The 55-year-old woman's neighbors blame her", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the director of the city administration say at the end of the news report?", "options": ["A) It will take time to solve the rat problem.", "B) All wild animals should be well protected.", "C) The woman was not to blame for the situation.", "D) No one should go unpunished for violating law."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It will take time to solve the rat problem.\n【定位】Anthony Moretti, director of the city administration, said he saw more than 20 rats near the\nwoman's home. He said it will take months to get the problem under control.\n市政管理负责人安东尼·莫雷蒂说，他在这位女士家附近看到了 20 多只老鼠。他说要把问题\n控制住还需要几个月时间。\n【信号】末段官员引语。He said it will take months to get the problem under control. 出现在新\n闻最后一段、由 director of the city administration 的引语收束，是题干「at the end」+\n「director said」两条线索的直接落点。\n【替换】it will take months to get the problem under control ↔ It will take time to solve the rat problem.\nmonths≈time, under control≈solve\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nAll wild animals should be well protected. 借用了录音里多次出现的 wild animals 做诱\n饵，但官员从未谈论「应保护所有野生动物」这种价值口号，他只是描述老鼠数量和耗时，\nAll wild animals 属于绝对化范围扩大。\n借用原文：It is prohibited to feed any wild animals including birds in Rhode Island City.\nC) 与原文相反\nThe woman was not to blame for the situation. 与录音传递的态度相反——录音\nNevertheless, while the woman's intention was to feed birds, it is clear that rats were\nalso benefiting. 明确指出「尽管她本意是喂鸟，但老鼠也因此得利」，是把责任与她挂\n钩，而非为她开脱。\n借用原文：Nevertheless, while the woman's intention was to feed birds, it is clear that rats were\nalso benefiting.\nD) 张冠李戴\nNo one should go unpunished for violating law. 看似呼应录音里 It is prohibited to\nfeed any wild animals 的法律口吻，但这是新闻叙述者的背景交代，并非「director of\nthe city administration」在末尾说的话，且 No one should go unpunished 这种绝对化\n执法口号录音中并未出现。\n借用原文：It is prohibited to feed any wild animals including birds in Rhode Island City.\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 To prepare for eventually sending astronauts to Mars, NASA began taking applications Friday for four\npeople to live for a year in Mars Dune Alpha. That's a 1,700-square-foot Martian habitat inside a\nbuilding in Houston.\n为了准备最终将宇航员送往火星,NASA于周五开始接受四人申请,让他们在Mars Dune Alpha中生活一\n年。这是位于休斯顿一座建筑内的一个1700平方英尺的火星栖息地。\n2 The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars. They will have limited\ncommunications with family, restricted food and resources.\n这些有偿志愿者将在类似火星的环境中工作。他们与家人的通讯将受到限制,食物和资源也会受限。\n3 NASA is planning three experiments with the first one starting in the fall next year. Food will all be\nready-to-eat space food. Some plants will be grown, but not potatoes like in the movie The Martian.\nNASA正在计划三次实验,第一次实验将于明年秋季开始。食物将全部是即食太空食品。会种植一些植物,但\n不会像电影《火星救援》中那样种植土豆。\n4 \"We want to understand how humans perform in them,\" said lead scientist Grace Douglas. \"We are\nlooking at Mars-realistic situations.\"\n首席科学家Grace Douglas说:\"我们想了解人类在这些环境中的表现。我们关注的是符合火星实际情况的\n场景。\"\n5 The application process opens Friday and they're not seeking just anybody. The requirements are strict,\nincluding a master's degree in a science, engineering or math field or pilot experience. Only American\ncitizens or permanent U.S. residents are acceptable. Applicants must be between 30 and 55 and in good\nphysical health.\n申请流程于周五开放,他们寻找的并非普通人选。要求非常严格,包括拥有科学、工程或数学领域的硕士\n学位或飞行员经验。只接受美国公民或美国永久居民。申请人必须年龄在30至55岁之间,身体健康状况\n良好。\n6 Attitude is key, said former Canadian astronaut Chris Hadfield. He said the participants need to be\nsuper competent, resourceful, and not relying on other people to feel comfortable.\n加拿大前宇航员Chris Hadfield表示,态度是关键。他说参与者需要非常能干、足智多谋,不依赖他人也能感\n到自在。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does NASA require the paid volunteers to do?", "options": ["A) Communicate with astronauts in Mars Dune Alpha.", "B) Work in an environment resembling Mars.", "C) Build a Martian habitat in Houston.", "D) Send in applications before Friday."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Work in an environment resembling Mars.\n【定位】The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars.\n这些带薪志愿者将会在一个与火星类似的环境中工作。\n【信号】题干主语原词复现。题干主体是 the paid volunteers，录音第 2 段开头 The paid\nvolunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars. 一字不差地把这个主语再说一\n遍，且紧跟着就给出动作 will work in an environment similar to Mars——这是新闻题最\n典型的答案信号：题干里的主语在录音里原词出现的那一句，往往就是答案句。\n【替换】will work in an environment similar to Mars ↔ Work in an environment resembling Mars\nsimilar to 替换 resembling\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项里出现了录音原词 Mars Dune Alpha，但录音从头到尾没说志愿者要去和宇航员通\n讯，只在第 2 段提了 They will have limited communications with family——是和家人\n有限通讯，不是和宇航员通讯。把 Mars Dune Alpha 这个专名扣到一个原文没说的动作上\n骗你听到原词就选。\n借用原文：Mars Dune Alpha\nC)\n录音说 a 1,700-square-foot Martian habitat inside a building in Houston，确实有\nMartian habitat 和 Houston 两个原词，但志愿者是住进去而不是建。选项 Build a\nMartian habitat in Houston 把 habitat 和 Houston 拼到 Build 这个动词上，是把建造者\n的事安到志愿者头上，典型张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a 1,700-square-foot Martian habitat inside a building in Houston\nD)\n录音第 1 段念了 NASA began taking applications Friday，第 5 段也说 The application\nprocess opens Friday——Friday 这个词真的反复出现，但说的是 NASA 周五开始收申\n请，而不是 NASA 要求志愿者周五前递申请。Friday 是被盗用来骗 听到就想选 的原词。\n借用原文：NASA began taking applications Friday", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What will the participants in the project eat?", "options": ["A) Ready-made food.", "B) Food that is organic.", "C) Food they grow.", "D) Potatoes mostly."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Ready-made food.\n【定位】Food will all be ready-to-eat space food. Some plants will be grown, but not potatoes like in\nthe movie The Martian.\n食物将全部是即食的太空食品。有一些植物会被种植，但不是像电影《火星救援》里那样的\n土豆。\n【信号】Food 起首+all 强调。录音第 3 段那一句 Food will all be ready-to-eat space food. 以题\n干关键词 Food 起首，且用 all 这种范围最大、最绝对的修饰词强调全部都是即食太空食品\n——主语+all+表语结构在听力里几乎等同于这就是答案，要立刻锁。紧接着 but not\npotatoes 又用 but 否定了土豆，进一步把答案聚焦在即食食物上。\n【替换】ready-to-eat space food ↔ Ready-made food\nready-to-eat=ready-made\nFood will all be ↔ Ready-made food\nall 等于唯一\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音从头到尾没有出现 organic 这个词，也没说食物是天然有机的；选项 Food that is\norganic. 是凭电影或常识对火星实验食物的想象，属于录音没说就硬塞的未提及干扰。\nC)\n录音确实念了 Some plants will be grown，grown 这个原词被选项 Food they grow. 直\n接盗用；但原文用 Some 限定只是一些，且强调 Food will all be ready-to-eat——食物全\n部是即食的，植物种植只是次要补充，不是吃的来源。听到 grown 就想选 C 是这道题最大\n的诱惑。\n借用原文：Some plants will be grown\nD)\n录音里 but not potatoes like in the movie The Martian 明确用 but not 否定了土豆，选\n项 Potatoes mostly. 却把它当作主食呈现，与原文方向完全相反。potatoes 这个原词被盗\n来诱惑那些被电影《火星救援》带偏的考生。\n借用原文：but not potatoes like in the movie The Martian", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do the news reports say about the Japanese mayor Takashi Kawamura?", "options": ["A) He apologized for scratching an athlete's gold medal.", "B) He was asked to present a gold medal to Miu Goto.", "C) He bit a softball player's Olympic gold medal.", "D) He attracted public attention at a media event."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He bit a softball player's Olympic gold medal.\n【定位】A Japanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a\nsoftball player.\n一位日本市长在周四为咬了一名垒球运动员的奥运金牌一事道歉。\n【信号】新闻首句即考点。新闻类录音的第一句往往就是核心事件概述，本题答案直接落在 A\nJapanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a\nsoftball player. 这句新闻开篇句上，biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player\n与 C 选项几乎逐词对应。\n【替换】biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player ↔ bit a softball player's Olympic gold medal\n动名词换过去式\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 项说他为 scratching（刮花）金牌道歉，但录音原文 apologized Thursday for biting\n道歉的对象是 bit（咬），不是 scratch。scratch 一词反而出现在第 7 段 I would not\nscratch it，是另一位运动员说的话，属于张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I handle my own gold medal so gently that I would not scratch it\nB) 与原文相反\nB 项说市长被要求把金牌颁给 Miu Goto，但录音 He asked her to put the medal around\nhis neck. 恰恰相反——是市长请运动员把奖牌挂在他自己脖子上，方向颠倒。\n借用原文：He asked her to put the medal around his neck.\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项说他在 media event 上吸引了公众关注。录音 during a public media event 确实出\n现 media event，但'吸引公众关注'是事件附带的结果，不是新闻报道的核心事件，与题干\nWhat do the news reports say about… 的核心不符。\n借用原文：Nagoya mayor Takashi Kawamura had praised an athlete, Miu Goto, during a public\nmedia event.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did mayor Takashi Kawamura offer to do?", "options": ["A) Have another picture taken with the Olympic medalist.", "B) Apologize to the International Olympic Committee.", "C) Get the damaged medal repaired.", "D) Pay for the cost of a new medal."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Pay for the cost of a new medal.\n【定位】The mayor said the medal was undamaged, though he offered to pay for the cost of a\nnew one.\n市长表示金牌并未受损，不过他主动提出愿意承担一枚新奖牌的费用。\n【信号】题干原词直接命中。录音里 he offered to pay for the cost of a new one 直接出现题干的\n核心动词 offered，紧接其后的 to pay for the cost of a new one 就是承诺动作。同时\nthough（转折）也是答案信号，提示前半句的 undamaged 是次要、后半句才是落点。\n【替换】offered to pay for the cost of a new one ↔ Pay for the cost of a new medal.\n代词还原为名词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项说再拍一张照。录音里确实出现 photographers（第 2 段），但市长并没有提出'再拍\n一张'，A 是无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项说他要向 International Olympic Committee 道歉。录音 Goto, however, has\naccepted the International Olympic Committee's offer of a replacement 里确实出现\nInternational Olympic Committee，但那是奥委会向运动员提供替换奖牌，与市长的道歉\n对象无关。市长道歉对象是 reporters（Kawamura told reporters Thursday）。\n借用原文：Goto, however, has accepted the International Olympic Committee's offer of\na replacement\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项预设奖牌已 damaged 需要修，但录音 the medal was undamaged 明确说市长称奖\n牌并未受损，故无需修复。前缀 un- 一字之差，方向完全相反。\n借用原文：the medal was undamaged", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did some Olympians say they would do with their medals?", "options": ["A) Allow no one to touch them.", "B) See them as symbols of honor.", "C) Treat them as treasures.", "D) Keep them in a safe place."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Treat them as treasures.\n【定位】Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures and that it was disrespectful and\nunacceptable for Kawamura to bite one.\n一些奥运选手表示，他们把自己的奖牌视若珍宝，川村咬奖牌的行为是不尊重的、不可接\n受的。\n【信号】题干引述结构对位。录音 Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures 几\n乎与题干 Some Olympians say they would do with their medals 一一对应，Some\nOlympians said 这个引述信号直接把答案句标了出来，紧随其后的 treat their medals as\ntreasures 即处理方式。\n【替换】they treat their medals as treasures ↔ Treat them as treasures.\n近乎原词复现\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项说不让任何人碰。录音里没有任何一句话说'不允许任何人触碰'，运动员只是说视若珍\n宝、会轻拿轻放（I handle my own gold medal so gently），并没有提到 touch 这件\n事。\nB) 过度推断\nB 项把奖牌看作 symbols of honor（荣誉象征）。录音里运动员说的是 treat as treasures\n（视若珍宝）和 handle gently（轻拿轻放），并没有上升到'荣誉象征'这种抽象概念，属于\n过度推断。\nD) 未提及\nD 项说放在 safe place（安全的地方）。录音通篇没有提到把奖牌放在哪里这一存放地点信\n息，运动员的表述只关乎'如何对待'与'如何轻拿轻放'，不涉及存放位置。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What did the woman do to her computer?", "options": ["A) She covered its screen with a plastic sheet.", "B) She connected it with her smartphone.", "C) She decorated it with colorful stickers.", "D) She bought some new software for it."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She covered its screen with a plastic sheet.\n【定位】It's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light.\n这是一张能阻挡蓝光的塑料薄膜。\n【信号】被问者对 What's that 的直接应答。男士开口就问 What's that orange thing on your\ncomputer screen?，女士紧接着在 It's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light. 里给出直接\n回答。\"问句紧跟答句\"是对话题最强的答案信号——被问者的下一句就是命题点。\n【替换】plastic sheet that blocks blue light ↔ plastic sheet\n选项原词复现自原文\nI have one that I use to cover my phone screen too ↔ covered its screen with a plastic sheet\nuse to cover 改写为 covered with\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 选项把 She connected it with her smartphone.\"把电脑连到手机\"塞进来——但原文只\n是说 I have one that I use to cover my phone screen too，提到 phone 仅仅是说\"我手\n机屏幕上也贴了一张\"，根本没有\"把电脑连到手机\"的动作。\n借用原文：I have one that I use to cover my phone screen too\nC) 偷换概念\nC 把女士的动作改成了 She decorated it with colorful stickers.\"用彩色贴纸装饰\"，原文\nIt's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light. 说的是\"阻挡蓝光的塑料膜\"，目的在于功能、不\n是装饰，颜色（橙）只是膜本身颜色而不是\"贴纸\"。\n借用原文：It's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light.\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 把她买的东西说成 She bought some new software for it.\"新软件\"，可原文清楚说 It's\nsomething I bought yesterday. It's a plastic sheet——买的是\"塑料膜\"这一实物，不是软\n件。\n借用原文：It's something I bought yesterday.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say about blue light?", "options": ["A) It may not be simply blue.", "B) It includes unnatural light.", "C) It is more harmful to young people.", "D) It induces people to fall asleep."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It includes unnatural light.\n【定位】Blue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural, for example, from\ncomputers, phones, televisions and other electronic devices.\n蓝光既包含自然光，也包含非自然光，比如来自电脑、手机、电视和其他电子设备的光。\n【信号】but … also includes 的修正信号。女士先给一个\"轻\"的说法 Blue light includes natural\nlight，紧跟着 but it also includes light that isn't natural 用 but 修正——听力中 but 之\n后才是命题点。这个\"先轻后重\"的修正结构是判定答案句的关键信号。\n【替换】light that isn't natural ↔ unnatural light\nisn't natural=unnatural\nBlue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural ↔\nIt includes unnatural light\n也包含=includes\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nIt may not be simply blue.\"它可能不只是蓝色\"说的是\"颜色不只蓝\"，可录音从未谈过蓝\n光\"颜色\"的复杂性，谈的是\"光源\"是否自然。两者讨论的层面不同。\n借用原文：It isn't that simple.\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音中提到 Poor sleep might be a problem for older people, but surely young\npeople can handle late nights. 是\"男士\"的看法，谈的是\"年轻人 vs 老人扛夜\"，并不是女\n士对\"蓝光对年轻人更有害\"的表态；女士全程没说蓝光对年轻人特别更糟。\n借用原文：Poor sleep might be a problem for older people, but surely young people can handle\nlate nights.\nD) 与原文相反\nIt induces people to fall asleep. 说蓝光\"让人入睡\"，可女士明确表态 making it harder\nfor us to fall asleep——蓝光过多反而让人更难入睡，方向完全相反。\n借用原文：making it harder for us to fall asleep", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why does the man say he hasn't slept enough for months?", "options": ["A) He has had much trouble falling asleep.", "B) He has had some sort of health problems.", "C) He has stayed up playing computer games.", "D) He has been burdened with excessive work."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has been burdened with excessive work.\n【定位】I haven't slept enough in months because I have too much work to do, and I feel fine.\n我已经好几个月没睡够了，因为要做的工作太多，可我感觉挺好的。\n【信号】because 的因果信号。男士在 I haven't slept enough in months because I have too\nmuch work to do 里用 because 直接给出原因——\"工作太多\"。because 是 Why 题最经\n典的答案信号，听到就直接锁。\n【替换】too much work to do ↔ been burdened with excessive work\ntoo much=excessive\nI have too much work to do ↔ He has been burdened with excessive work\nhave work to do=be burdened with work\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nHe has had much trouble falling asleep. 是\"努力睡却睡不着\"，可男士说的是 I haven't\nslept enough in months because I have too much work to do——他不是睡不着，而是\n没时间睡。 trouble falling asleep（失眠）和 not having time to sleep（没时间睡）是两\n回事。\n借用原文：making it harder for us to fall asleep\nB) 偷换概念\nHe has had some sort of health problems. 暗示\"有健康问题所以没睡够\"，可原文里男士\n反而说 I feel fine——他根本没承认自己有健康问题；况且原文谈\"健康问题\"是作为\"长期缺\n睡可能导致的后果\"出现，不是男士没睡够的原因。\n借用原文：not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems\nC) 原词陷阱\nHe has stayed up playing computer games. 借 computer 这个录音里反复出现的原词制\n造\"听见 computer 就想选\"的诱惑。但原文里 computer 都用于 I'm on my computer\nvery late most nights 等场合——他熬夜是在\"用电脑工作\"，根本没说在\"玩游戏\"。\n借用原文：I'm on my computer very late most nights", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What has the woman learned from the research she has read?", "options": ["A) Exposure to blue light is the chief cause of obesity.", "B) Sleep may be more important than people assumed.", "C) Sleep may also be negatively affected by natural light.", "D) Overuse of electronic devices may cause heart disease."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Sleep may be more important than people assumed.\n【定位】Well, the research I've read shows that sleep is probably even more important than we\nthought, and that not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems like\nobesity and heart disease.\n嗯，我读到的研究表明，睡眠很可能比我们以为的还要重要，而睡眠不足可能导致肥胖和心\n脏病等严重的健康问题。\n【信号】the research shows that 的结论句框。女士开口就用 the research I've read shows that\n这一典型结论句框启动答案句，紧跟着的 sleep is probably even more important than\nwe thought 就是研究的核心结论。这是听力里最稳的命题信号之一。\n【替换】even more important than we thought ↔ more important than people assumed\nwe thought=people assumed\nsleep is probably even more important ↔ Sleep may be more important\nprobably=may\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文 not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems like obesity\nand heart disease 只说\"缺睡可能导致肥胖等问题\"，主语是\"缺睡\"，且用 contribute to 这\n种有限度的因果措辞；A 把锅甩到 Exposure to blue light 头上，并升级为 the chief\ncause of obesity 这种独占性主因——既偷换了主语，又夸大了因果强度。\n借用原文：not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems like obesity and\nheart disease\nC) 与原文相反\n原文反复说 artificial blue light from electronic devices 干扰睡眠，矛头是\"非自然 / 人\n造\"蓝光；C Sleep may also be negatively affected by natural light. 偏偏把矛头扭到\nnatural light，与原文方向完全相反。\n借用原文：all the artificial blue light from electronic devices means we have to try harder to\nsleep well\nD) 偷换概念\n原文把 heart disease 仅作为 serious health problems like obesity and heart disease\n的举例之一，主语是\"缺睡\"；D Overuse of electronic devices may cause heart disease.\n把主语换成\"过度使用电子设备\"、把举例\"心脏病\"提到结论位置，既偷换主语又拉直因果\n链。\n借用原文：not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems like obesity and\nheart disease", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What question were both speakers fed up with when they were kids?", "options": ["A) What they wanted to be when grown up.", "B) What their favorite cartoon character was.", "C) What they learned from computer games.", "D) What they liked to do most after school."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） What they wanted to be when grown up.\n【定位】And I got sick every time adults asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up.\n每次大人问我长大后想做什么，我都会觉得烦透了。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答信号 got sick。女问 did you know what job you wanted to do when\nyou grew up? 后，男方在第二段用 I got sick every time adults asked me what I\nwanted to be when I grew up. 直接回应，got sick 就是『厌烦』这一情绪的最强信号；\n紧接着第三段女方又说 It's the same with me. 表示自己也一样，双方共同厌烦的那个问题\n就此锁定。\n【替换】what I wanted to be when I grew up ↔ What they wanted to be when grown up.\n人称与时态同义改写\n【排除】B)\n整段对话从未出现 cartoon 或具体的卡通形象名称，What their favorite cartoon\ncharacter was. 属于无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第七段 spending 10 hours a day playing computer games 真的念了 computer\ngames，但语境是女方说『家长希望孩子放学别只打游戏』，与『被问烦了的童年问题』完\n全无关；选项 What they learned from computer games. 借用了 computer games 这个\n原词来骗『听到就想选』。\n借用原文：spending 10 hours a day playing computer games\nD) 偷换概念\n对话谈的是『长大后想做什么』，没有出现 after school 这种放学时段的具体话题，What\nthey liked to do most after school. 属于偷换概念，把『长大目标』换成了『日常活\n动』。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What occupation do adults see as both sensible and ambitious according to the woman?", "options": ["A) A stock broker.", "B) A pop singer.", "C) A mechanical engineer.", "D) A basketball player."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A mechanical engineer.\n【定位】My daughter's stock answers are basketball player, pop singer, mechanical engineer. Adults\nlove that last one, as it's the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious.\n我女儿的口头答案就是篮球运动员、流行歌手、机械工程师。大人们最喜欢最后那一个，因\n为它把『靠谱』和『有抱负』完美地结合在一起。\n【信号】指代锁定 + 因果信号 as。原文用 that last one 指代列举里排在最后的 mechanical\nengineer，紧接着 as 引出原因 the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious，直\n接对应题干 both sensible and ambitious 两个条件，是典型的『指代+因果』双重信号\n锁定。\n【替换】that last one ↔ A mechanical engineer.\n顺位指代还原\nthe perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious ↔ A mechanical engineer.\n属性匹配题干修饰\n【排除】A)\n整段录音从未出现 stock broker 这个职业，A stock broker. 属无中生有，可能是利用『一\n听就显得 ambitious』的常识脑补干扰。\nB)\n录音里确实念到 pop singer，但它只是被列举的三个选项之一，并不是大人 love 的那一\n项；A pop singer. 借原词诱选，盗用了 pop singer 这个词。\n借用原文：pop singer\nD)\nbasketball player 也是被女方提到的列举项之一，但同样不是大人最喜欢那一项，A\nbasketball player. 也是利用原词诱选。\n借用原文：basketball player", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What kind of goal-setting does the woman like to see in children?", "options": ["A) Ambitious.", "B) Sensible.", "C) Imaginative.", "D) Practical."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Imaginative.\n【定位】When she was much younger, my daughter used to say she wanted to be Queen of the Clouds,\nwhich I loved. That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children, springing from their\nboundless imaginations.\n在我女儿小得多的时候，她常说她想当『云之女王』，我当时特别喜欢。那才是我喜欢看到\n孩子们立的那种目标，是从他们无边的想象力里长出来的。\n【信号】原话直引 the kind of goal-setting I like 与原因 springing from imaginations。原文\n女方直接说 That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children，几乎和题干一字不\n差；后半句 springing from their boundless imaginations 给出立目标的核心属性来自\n『无边想象力』，是定位的关键信号。\n【替换】springing from their boundless imaginations ↔ Imaginative.\n名词到形容词的同源替换\nQueen of the Clouds ↔ Imaginative.\n具体例子到抽象形容词的概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里出现 the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious，ambitious 确被念到，\n但语境是『大人对 mechanical engineer 的评价』，不是女方喜欢的立目标类型，\nAmbitious. 借原词诱选。\n借用原文：the ambitious\nB) 原词陷阱\nthe sensible 同样出现在大人评价 mechanical engineer 那句里，并非女方喜欢的立目标\n类型；Sensible. 又是借原词诱选。\n借用原文：the sensible\nD)\n女方明确表达自己喜欢的是『来自想象力』的目标，而 Practical. 偏务实，恰是和『想象\n力』相反的方向；男方第四段还说 we grown-ups can be tedious and limiting in our\nneed for reality，说明双方都批评『太务实』，所以 Practical 与女方立场相反。\n借用原文：we grown-ups can be tedious and limiting in our need for reality", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman suggest adults should do?", "options": ["A) Relax their strict control of their kids.", "B) Help their kids understand themselves.", "C) Impose their own dreams on their kids.", "D) Dismiss their high expectations of their kids."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Help their kids understand themselves.\n【定位】We should be helping our kids understand who they are, even if that means letting go of who\nwe think they should be.\n我们应该帮助孩子明白他们究竟是谁，哪怕这意味着要放下我们心里想让他们成为的样子。\n【信号】we should be+建议动作信号。女方在最后一段用 We should be helping our kids\nunderstand who they are 直接说出建议，we should 是最典型的建议信号；位置又在对\n话最后，是收束式落点，几乎可以确定就是这道建议题的答案句。\n【替换】helping our kids understand who they are ↔ Help their kids understand themselves.\n宾语从句到反身代词的等值改写\n【排除】A)\n录音并未谈到家长应放松对孩子的『严格控制』，只是说不要把自己的期望强加给孩子；\nRelax their strict control of their kids. 把『放下自己的预设』偷换成『放松严格控制』，\n扩大了原文范围。\nC)\nletting go of who we think they should be 表明女方主张『放下我们希望他们成为的样\n子』，等于反对把自己的梦强加给孩子；Impose their own dreams on their kids. 与原文\n立场完全相反，是反向陷阱。\n借用原文：letting go of who we think they should be\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第八段提到 false expectations，expectations 是原词，但语境是『问 What do you\nwant to be 会带来虚假期望和失望』，并非要求家长 dismiss their high expectations；\n选项 Dismiss their high expectations of their kids. 借 expectations 这个原词诱选，又\n把『不要制造虚假期望』推断成『要彻底抛弃所有高期望』，过度推断。\n借用原文：false expectations", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What are a number of phone companies trying to do in Africa?", "options": ["A) Set up company branches.", "B) Improve its infrastructure.", "C) Introduce iPhones into its markets.", "D) Promote Internet-ready phones."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Promote Internet-ready phones.\n【定位】Now, some phone companies are trying to introduce Internet-ready phones into\nAfrican markets.\n如今，一些手机公司正试图将能上网的手机引入非洲市场。\n【信号】句首 Now 引出新话题。Now, some phone companies are trying to introduce\nInternet-ready phones into African markets. 里句首 Now 是典型的话题切换信号，把镜\n头从'非洲上网难'切到'手机公司在做什么'，紧跟其后的就是题目要问的具体动作。\n【替换】introduce Internet-ready phones ↔ Promote Internet-ready phones.\nintroduce≈promote 同义替换\ninto African markets ↔ Promote Internet-ready phones.\nin Africa = into African markets\n【排除】A) 未提及\nSet up company branches. 在录音里根本没出现 set up 或 branches 这类词，是凭'公司\n+非洲'凑出的常识推论，属于未提及类干扰。\nB)\nImprove its infrastructure. 借了 tough to wire for Internet access 这类'基建落后'的语境\n制造既视感，但原文从未说手机公司去改善基础设施，而是说他们另辟蹊径推廉价手机，是\n典型的原词陷阱+偷换概念。\n借用原文：Most Africans live in rural areas that are tough to wire for Internet access.\nC)\nIntroduce iPhones into its markets. 盗用了原文段尾 Apple's top-of-the-line iPhone\nhas $390 worth of stuff. 这一处 iPhone 原词，但原文恰恰是把 iPhone 当成对照——非\n洲推的是低价 KaiOS 手机、不是 iPhone，这是典型原词陷阱+与原文相反。\n借用原文：Apple's top-of-the-line iPhone has $390 worth of stuff.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "How do KaiOS smartphones differ from smartphones of most other companies?", "options": ["A) They cater to Africans' needs.", "B) They are more expensive models.", "C) They are more powerful and capable", "D) They boast the longest battery life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They cater to Africans' needs.\n【定位】Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable. But KaiOS went\nthe other way. It made every effort to keep the essential capabilities of smartphones, but strip\nout costs and preserve battery life for people who likely have inadequate access to electricity.\n大多数公司都在努力让手机越来越强大、功能越来越全。但 KaiOS 反其道而行：它竭力保\n留智能手机的核心功能，同时砍掉成本、保住续航，服务那些用电不稳的人群。\n【信号】But + went the other way 双重转折。But KaiOS went the other way. 里的 But 与\nwent the other way 是典型的双重转折信号，前一句 Most companies are trying to\nmake phones ever more powerful and capable. 是被否定的对照基线，转折后那一句才\n是真正在讲 KaiOS 的差异点。\n【替换】strip out costs and preserve battery life for people who likely have inadequate access to electricity\n↔ They cater to Africans' needs.\n服务用电不稳人群→迎合非洲人需求\nBut KaiOS went the other way. ↔ They cater to Africans' needs.\n反其道而行=另辟需求侧\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey are more expensive models. 借了 more expensive models 这个原词，但原文恰恰\n是说 KaiOS 给出更便宜的替代品、绕开 more expensive models，与原文方向完全相反，\n是原词陷阱+与原文相反。\n借用原文：The KaiOS devices offer an alternative to the more expensive models that remain out of\nreach of many Africans\nC)\nThey are more powerful and capable 直接照搬了 more powerful and capable 这串原\n词，但原文是说'多数公司在追求更强更全，而 KaiOS 反其道而行'，KaiOS 恰恰不是更强更\n全，原词陷阱+张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable.\nD)\nThey boast the longest battery life. 盗用了 preserve battery life 这一处续航话题，但原\n文只说'保住/延长续航'，并没有说'最长'，longest 是程度的过度推断，未提及 + 与原文相\n反。\n借用原文：preserve battery life for people who likely have inadequate access to electricity", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What are KaiOS smartphones equipped with?", "options": ["A) A large touchscreen.", "B) An old-school keypad.", "C) A voice-response device.", "D) A digitally-designed system."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） An old-school keypad.\n【定位】Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad.\n它们不用触摸屏，而是用老式键盘来操控。\n【信号】Instead of 一句给出'不是A、是B'。Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with\nan old-school keypad. 用 Instead of 这一对比信号，把两种硬件方案并排摆出来：前半否\n定 touchscreen、后半肯定 old-school keypad，结构上一句话同时回答了'不是什么+是\n什么'。\n【替换】they're controlled with an old-school keypad ↔ An old-school keypad.\n原词级同义对应\n【排除】A)\nA large touchscreen. 盗用了原文 Instead of a touchscreen 的 touchscreen 原词，但原\n文用 Instead of 明确否定了 touchscreen，方向完全反了，是典型原词陷阱+与原文相反。\n借用原文：Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad.\nC)\nA voice-response device. 在录音里完全没有 voice 或 response 类词，是凭'智能手机也\n可能用语音控制'这种常识脑补的，属于未提及。\nD)\nA digitally-designed system. 与原文里描述 KaiOS 手机外观、控制方式的句子毫无对\n应，'数字化设计的系统'是个含义空泛的兜底干扰，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What is said about using recycled plastic to make plastic products in the past?", "options": ["A) It ensured sustainable economic growth.", "B) It was strongly opposed by manufacturers.", "C) It was cheaper than using fossil fuel plastic.", "D) It satisfied consumer demands on the whole."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It was cheaper than using fossil fuel plastic.\n【定位】For years, using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap. By contrast, fossil fuel\nplastic was more expensive. Thus this sustainable option was an economic option too.\n多年来，用回收塑料制造塑料产品一直很便宜。相比之下，化石燃料塑料更贵。因此这种可\n持续的选项同时也是一种经济划算的选项。\n【信号】对比信号 By contrast 锁定答案句。答案句的关键信号是 By contrast——它紧跟在\"回收\n塑料便宜\"之后，接出\"化石燃料塑料更贵\"的对比，正好把两者的价格高低关系明确点出。此\n外开头的 For years 对应题干的 in the past，是时间锚点。\n【替换】using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap ↔\nIt was cheaper than using fossil fuel plastic.\n录音两句并一句·形容词转比较级\nfossil fuel plastic was more expensive ↔ than using fossil fuel plastic\n对比对象逐词对齐\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音原句是 this sustainable option was an economic option too，意思是\"这种可持续\n选项同时也是经济划算的选项\"，强调的是\"便宜=经济\"，与\"保证可持续经济增长\"完全不是\n一回事。选项把 sustainable + economic 这两个原词拼成 sustainable economic\ngrowth，但 growth（增长）在录音里根本没提。\n借用原文：this sustainable option was an economic option too\nB)\n录音第一段只讲了\"便宜\"和\"经济\"，全篇也未出现 manufacturers oppose 这类否定/反对\n的措辞，strongly opposed 是凭空捏造的反向描述，属于典型的\"未提及\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音确实提到 This may be because of consumer demands，但那是在解释\"现在回收塑\n料反而更贵\"的原因，发生在\"现在\"，与题干限定的 in the past 时间错位。选项盗用了\nconsumer demand 这个原词把\"现在\"的信息安到\"过去\"，是典型的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：This may be because of consumer demands", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What has led to more competitive price for new plastic?", "options": ["A) The capacity to mass produce it.", "B) The U.S. federal government's regulations.", "C) A boom in market demand for clear plastic bottles.", "D) A rapid increase in U.S. petroleum chemical production."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A rapid increase in U.S. petroleum chemical production.\n【定位】Meanwhile, new plastic is becoming cheaper. This is because of a boom in petroleum\nchemical production from the U.S.\n与此同时，新塑料正变得越来越便宜。这是因为美国的石油化工产量出现了大幅增长。\n【信号】因果信号 This is because 锁定答案句。答案句靠 This is because of 这个清晰的因果信号\n锁定——录音里出现\"new plastic is becoming cheaper\"（结果）后紧接着用 This is\nbecause of 给出原因 a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S.，这就\n是题目要的原因。\n【替换】a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S. ↔\nA rapid increase in U.S. petroleum chemical production.\nboom=rapid increase·同义词替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n\"大规模生产能力\"听起来很合理（产能高自然成本低），但录音里从未提到 capacity 或\nmass produce，这是凭常识脑补出的答案，属于未提及。\nB)\n录音里 U.S. 只与 petroleum chemical production from the U.S. 搭配，是讲\"美国石化产\n量\"，全篇并未提到 U.S. federal government 的任何 regulation。选项盗用了原词 U.S.，\n又把后半段 The UK government 的\"政府监管\"语义错位拼到\"美国\"头上，是典型的\"原词\n+张冠李戴\"混合干扰。\n借用原文：petroleum chemical production from the U.S.\nC) 偷换概念\nclear plastic bottles 确实在 Makers of clear plastic bottles may also opt for new\nfossil-fuel based plastic to save money 里出现过，但那是讲\"透明塑料瓶的厂家会选新塑\n料\"——是\"新塑料更便宜\"的结果之一，而不是它变便宜的原因。选项把\"结果\"包装成\"原\n因\"。\n借用原文：Makers of clear plastic bottles may also opt for new fossil-fuel based plastic to\nsave money", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the UK government plan to do about plastic?", "options": ["A) Require companies to use 30% of new plastic.", "B) Increase the supply of new plastic in the market.", "C) Reduce the amount of plastic pollution in local areas.", "D) Take measures to promote the use of recycled plastic."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Take measures to promote the use of recycled plastic.\n【定位】The UK government plans to tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in\ntheir products. Additionally, the government is planning to increase the quantity of recycled\nplastic in the market.\n英国政府计划对那些产品中回收塑料使用比例不足30%的公司征税。此外，政府还计划增加\n市场上回收塑料的数量。\n【信号】主体+计划信号 plans to / is planning to 双重锁定。本段同时出现两次明显的\"计划\"信\n号：The UK government plans to 与 the government is planning to——主体+plan to\n的合体两次出现，构成两条并列计划，是无可争议的答案句。\n【替换】tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic ↔\nTake measures to promote the use of recycled plastic.\n具体措施→抽象概括\nincrease the quantity of recycled plastic in the market ↔ promote the use of recycled plastic\n增加供应=促进使用·名词化与动词化对调\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音原文是 tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic，30% 限定的\n是 recycled plastic（回收塑料），政府要求的是\"至少用30%的回收塑料\"。选项把数字\n30% 原样搬过来，但偷偷把 recycled plastic 换成 new plastic（新塑料），方向完全反了\n——典型的\"数字原词陷阱+正话反说\"。\n借用原文：tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in their products\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说政府计划 increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market——增加的是回\n收塑料的量。选项把 recycled plastic 偷换成 new plastic（新塑料），把\"增加回收塑料供\n应\"扭曲成\"增加新塑料供应\"，正好相反。又是把 increase 原词留着、偷换名词的伎俩。\n借用原文：the government is planning to increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market\nC)\n录音里 reduce plastic pollution 的目标是 reducing the enormous amount of plastic\npollution in the oceans——减少海洋里的塑料污染。选项把 oceans 偷换成 local areas\n（本地地区），范围被偷改了；并且 reduce pollution 这件事在录音里也不是政府计划的\n内容，而是\"包装厂家被施压\"去做的，主体也不对。属于范围偷改+张冠李戴的双重错。\n借用原文：reducing the enormous amount of plastic pollution in the oceans", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan?", "options": ["A) It studies dreams.", "B) It rents a place for nap-takers.", "C) It is a hotel for business people.", "D) It is a nap research institute."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It rents a place for nap-takers.\n【定位】At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan, naps are for sale. A 45-minute session in a\ndarkened enclosure with peace and quiet costs $25. To be clear, this institution is no hotel.\nThis is a nap joint.\n在曼哈顿的一家叫 Dreamery 的店里,午睡是被拿来出售的。在一个黑暗、安静的小隔间里\n睡 45 分钟,要 25 美元。说清楚一点,这家机构不是酒店,这是一家专门的午睡店。\n【信号】正面定义 + 否定式澄清。答案句的信号有两层:一是 naps are for sale 直接给出 Dreamery\n做什么生意——卖午睡;二是 To be clear, this institution is no hotel. This is a nap joint.\n用'no… this is…'的否定式定义把考生最容易误以为的身份(酒店)否掉、把真身(专门做午睡\n的店)亮出来,这是典型的篇章信号:说话人主动澄清的句子常常就是答案句。\n【替换】naps are for sale ↔ rents a place for nap-takers\nnaps are for sale ≈ rents a place for nap-takers\nA 45-minute session in a darkened enclosure with peace and quiet costs $25. ↔\nrents a place for nap-takers\na darkened enclosure ≈ a place;costs $25 ≈ rents\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt studies dreams. 利用机构名 Dreamery 含 dream 一词来制造'研究梦'的字面联想,但录\n音从未说这家店研究 dreams,只说它出售 naps are for sale;店名带 dream 不等于业务是研\n究 dream,这是把字面误当成业务定义。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is a hotel for business people. 里的 hotel 在录音 this institution is no hotel 里原词出\n现,但出现的位置正是被讲话人否掉的位置——讲话人明确说'这不是酒店'。考生若只听到\nhotel 三个字就选,会正好掉进讲话人提前设的反话陷阱里。\n借用原文：this institution is no hotel\nD) 过度推断\nIt is a nap research institute. 把这家店从'a business / a nap joint'拔高为'研究机构'。录\n音明明说 At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan,定位是 business(生意),又自称 nap\njoint(店);从未说它是 research institute,这是把性质从'商业'拔高到'学术研究',越界推断。\n借用原文：At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Why does the speaker ask us to look at the world around us at 2:30 in the afternoon?", "options": ["A) To find out creative people's work performance.", "B) To see how many people can go without napping.", "C) To understand the obvious importance of napping.", "D) To feel how difficult it is to get his idea across."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To understand the obvious importance of napping.\n【定位】Is a nap worth $25? The answer is obviously yes. Here, at this point in the argument, it's\ntraditional for me to bring up all the studies that show the benefits of napping. But do you\nreally need experts to tell you that? Just look at the world around you at 2:30 in the afternoon.\n午睡值不值 25 美元?答案显然是值的。讲到这一步,我按惯例本该搬出一堆研究来证明午睡\n的好处。但你真的需要专家来告诉你这件事吗?只要看看下午两点半你身边的世界就行了。\n【信号】立场句 + 反问 + 让你看现象。信号有三层:一是立场句 The answer is obviously yes. 先抛\n出讲话人的论点——午睡显然值得;二是反问 But do you really need experts to tell you\nthat?,讲话人用反问替代了'引研究'的常规论证;三是承接动作 Just look at the world\naround you at 2:30 in the afternoon.——既然不需要专家,那就用'你身边下午两点半的景\n象'来代替专家的研究,直接让听众自己看到午睡显然重要。三层连起来:让你看下午两点半的\n世界,目的就是让你自己印证讲话人那句'午睡显然重要'。\n【替换】The answer is obviously yes. ↔ the obvious importance of napping\nobviously yes ≈ obvious importance\nJust look at the world around you at 2:30 in the afternoon. ↔\nTo understand the obvious importance of napping\nlook at ≈ understand\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo find out creative people's work performance. 借了录音第三段里 I don't understand\nhow people have creative careers without napping. 中的 creative 一词制造熟悉感,但讲\n话人让人看下午两点半的世界,目的不是去研究'有创造力的人的工作表现',他根本没让你去观\n察任何特定人群。这是跨段盗词,把第三段的词嫁接到第二段的目的上。\n借用原文：I don't understand how people have creative careers without napping.\nB) 与原文相反\nTo see how many people can go without napping. 在方向上正好与讲话人立场相反。讲\n话人是在论证'午睡显然重要',让你看世界是为了证明大家都需要午睡;选项却说目的是'看有多\n少人不靠午睡也行',这把目的反向了。\n借用原文：The answer is obviously yes.\nD) 偷换概念\nTo feel how difficult it is to get his idea across. 把讲话人的目的偷换成'体会到自己讲清\n楚观点有多难'。录音里讲话人并不是在抱怨自己难以传达观点,他相反非常自信地说'你不需\n要专家'——他对自己的观点能不能被听懂没有任何困扰,选项是对讲话人姿态的歪曲。\n借用原文：But do you really need experts to tell you that?", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about the quantity and quality of the speaker's work?", "options": ["A) They decline due to pointless meetings.", "B) They depend on his ability to concentrate.", "C) They enable him to enjoy a creative career.", "D) They are affected by the overuse of social media."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They depend on his ability to concentrate.\n【定位】I've been working from home for more than 10 years now. And the quality and quantity of\nwork I can do emerges directly from my ability to concentrate.\n我在家工作已经超过 10 年了。我能完成的工作的质量和数量,直接取决于我集中注意力的\n能力。\n【信号】因果衔接词 emerges directly from。答案信号是 emerges directly from 这一因果衔接\n短语——它把'工作的质和量'与'集中力'明确绑成因果关系。这种'A\nemerges/comes/results from B'结构是听力细节题最直接的答案信号,因为它已经替你\n把'答案靠什么'说清楚了,只需把 B 抓住即可。\n【替换】the quality and quantity of work I can do ↔ They\nthe quality and quantity of work ≈ They\nemerges directly from my ability to concentrate ↔ depend on his ability to concentrate\nemerges directly from ≈ depend on\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey decline due to pointless meetings. 借了录音里 attend pointless meetings until 7\np.m. 中的 pointless meetings 制造熟悉感,但 pointless meetings 在录音里是讲话人用来\n描述'不午睡就要被迫做的烂事'之一,并不是导致讲话人产出 decline 的原因。讲话人压根没\n说自己产出在下降,只是把不午睡的人生活描述为低效。\n借用原文：attend pointless meetings until 7 p.m.\nC) 与原文相反\nThey enable him to enjoy a creative career. 盗了录音 I don't understand how people\nhave creative careers without napping. 中的 creative career 来诱选。但因果方向相反:\n录音是说'人怎么能在没午睡的情况下还做创造性工作'——讲话人是用 creative career 来反\n衬'没午睡就难做创造性工作',而不是说工作的质和量'使他能享受'创造性职业。选项把'我的\n工作量质 → 取决于集中力'偷换成'我的工作量质 → 让我能享受创造性职业',因果颠倒。\n借用原文：I don't understand how people have creative careers without napping.\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey are affected by the overuse of social media. 借了录音里 daydream and drift\naround social media 中的 social media 制造熟悉感。但录音里 drift around social\nmedia 是讲话人描述'不午睡会陷入'的散漫状态之一,并非他在说自己的工作量质被社交媒体\n过度使用所影响。同样是用原词嫁接到错位的因果上。\n借用原文：daydream and drift around social media", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say he finds odd?", "options": ["A) Some bosses associate napping with laziness.", "B) Many office workers nap during work hours.", "C) Some bosses can concentrate without napping.", "D) Many of his friends daydream in the office."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Some bosses associate napping with laziness.\n【定位】The friends I have who still work in offices inform me their bosses insist that they take the\nsecond option, and that napping is associated with laziness. I genuinely find it odd.\n我那些还在办公室上班的朋友告诉我,他们的老板坚持要他们走第二条路(发呆、刷社交媒\n体、开无意义的会),而且认为午睡跟懒惰挂钩。我真的觉得这件事很奇怪。\n【信号】态度词 odd 直接锁前一句。答案信号是 I genuinely find it odd. 中的 odd——题干 What\ndoes the speaker say he finds odd? 在录音里几乎是原词复现,it 又指代前面紧挨的那个现\n象。听力里'我觉得 odd / strange / surprising'这种句式,it 指的就是前一句讲过的事——\n这是态度题的最直接信号。\n【替换】their bosses insist that they take the second option, and that napping is associated with laziness ↔\nSome bosses associate napping with laziness.\ntheir bosses + napping is associated with laziness ≈ Some bosses associate napping with\nlaziness\nI genuinely find it odd. ↔ Some bosses associate napping with laziness.\nit = 前一句现象 ≈ A 项内容\n【排除】B) 未提及\nMany office workers nap during work hours. 借了录音里 The friends I have who still\nwork in offices 中的 offices 制造场景熟悉感,但录音从未说很多上班族在上班时间午睡,反\n而说老板逼他们走第二条路(发呆、刷社交媒体、开无意义的会)。这是凭空造一个录音没有\n的现象。\nC) 原词陷阱\nSome bosses can concentrate without napping. 盗了第三段 my ability to concentrate\n里的 concentrate 一词制造熟悉感,但讲话人本人的立场恰恰是'不午睡很难保持集中力',而且\n讲话人觉得 odd 的对象是'老板对午睡的偏见',不是老板的集中力。选项把讲话人不解的对象\n偷换成了与原话无关的、且方向相反的命题。\n借用原文：my ability to concentrate\nD) 张冠李戴\nMany of his friends daydream in the office. 盗了第三段 daydream and drift around\nsocial media 里的 daydream 与第四段 The friends I have who still work in offices 里的\nfriends,把两个原词拼到一起造一个录音没说的现象。录音里讲话人觉得 odd 的是老板把午\n睡当懒惰,而不是朋友在办公室发呆;且 daydream 是讲话人假设'不午睡就会陷入'的状态,不\n是他对朋友行为的描述。\n借用原文：daydream and drift around social media", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) sew\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列 + 不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】把布\"缝\"成衬衫形状，sew 最准\n语境是 create fabric and __(26)__ it into a shirt shape——造出布料再把它\"缝\"成衬衫形\n状，sew（缝制）正是做衣服的标准动作，与 into a shirt shape 搭配自然。\n【竞争词】H) mend 义偏\"修补\"非\"制作\"\nmend 是\"修补\"已破损的东西，这里是从布料\"从无到有做成\"衬衫，不是修补，方向不符。\nM) shrink 义为\"收缩\"，逻辑反\nshrink 是\"使缩小\"，\"把布缩成衬衫形状\"讲不通；而且 shrink 正是第 35 空\"水供应减少\"要用的\n词，留给那里。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) abundant\n形容词\n【词性槽】the most + 空 + 名词，形容词最高级作定语\n【依据】棉花是\"最常见/产量最大\"的衣料，abundant 最贴\n句意是棉花作为 the most __(27)__ clothing material——最\"丰富、大量\"的衣料，与下文\none of the most useful cash crops across the globe 呼应，abundant（丰富的、大量\n的）正合\"用得最广\"之意。\n【竞争词】E) exact 搭配不成立\nexact 意为\"确切的\"，\"最确切的衣料\"语义不通，exact 通常修饰数字、答案等，不能形容棉花作\n为材料的普遍性。\nG) intense 义不对\nintense 是\"强烈的、剧烈的\"，形容情感、光线、竞争等，\"最强烈的衣料\"讲不通；它更可能去\n配第 31 空那种\"产业\"语境，但此处不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) exact\n形容词\n【词性槽】to be + 空，系动词后作表语\n【依据】\"to be exact\"=确切地说，是固定插入语\n2,700 liters per shirt to be __(28)__ 在精确数字 2,700 liters 后用 \"to be exact\"（确切地\n说）这一固定表达收尾，exact 修饰前面给出的精确数字，框架直接锁死。\n【竞争词】G) intense 义不符且无此搭配\nintense 是\"强烈的\"，\"to be intense\"在此说不通，也不是固定插入语；它形容情感/程度，\n与\"确切数字\"语境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) statistics\n名词\n【词性槽】some more + 空 + about，形容词后接名词\n【依据】承接上句\"2,700 升\"的数字，statistics=统计数据最贴\n前句刚给出 2,700 liters per shirt 这样的数字，叫人去看视频了解\"更多令人震惊的数据\"，\nstatistics（统计数据）与 mind-blowing、about cotton production 完全吻合。\n【竞争词】C) awareness 义为\"意识\"，且被第 32 空占用\nawareness 是\"意识\"，\"更多令人震惊的意识\"不通；它是第 32 空 spread ___ 的正解，留给\n那里。\nD) conscience 义为\"良心\"，与数据无关\nconscience（良心、道德感）和\"关于棉花生产的震撼信息\"不搭，且不可用 some more 这样表\n数量地修饰。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) increasingly\n副词\n【词性槽】is + 空 + becoming，插在系动词与现在分词之间\n【依据】清洁水\"日益\"变得抢手，increasingly 表渐变正合\nClean water is __(30)__ becoming one of the most sought-after resources in the\nworld 描述一种正在加剧的趋势，increasingly（越来越、日益地）修饰 becoming 表渐\n变，语义贴切。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) textile\n名词\n【词性槽】the + 空 + and cotton industries，定语名词\n【依据】与 cotton 并列指\"纺织业\"，textile 正合\nthe __(31)__ and cotton industries——\"纺织与棉花产业\"，textile（纺织品/纺织的）和\ncotton 同属衣料产业、并列自然，且 textile industry 是固定说法。\n【竞争词】D) conscience 义为\"良心\"，不能与 cotton 并列指产业\nconscience 是抽象\"良心\"，\"良心和棉花产业\"语义不搭，也不构成 ___ industry 这类产业名，\n逻辑不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) awareness\n名词\n【词性槽】spread + 空 + of，及物动词后作宾语\n【依据】\"spread awareness of\"=传播……意识，固定搭配\nspread __(32)__ of how environmentally harmful cotton is——视频是为了\"传播对棉花\n危害环境的认知\"，spread awareness of 是高频固定搭配，awareness（意识、知晓）完\n全契合。\n【竞争词】D) conscience 形近义异，无此搭配\nconscience（良心）与 awareness（意识）形近易混，但\"spread conscience of\"不成立，句意\n是让人\"知道\"棉花的环境危害，是认知层面而非道德良心。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— conscience 与 awareness 形近、词性都对。spread\n__(32)__ of how environmentally harmful cotton is：D\\(conscience\\) 和正解形近、同\n为抽象名词，最易误选；防坑就背一句固定搭配\"spread awareness of\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) nearly\n副词\n【词性槽】空 + 40 percent，修饰数量的副词\n【依据】修饰 40 percent 表\"将近 40%\"，nearly 最贴\ncut down by __(33)__ 40 percent 说节水幅度\"将近 40%\"，nearly（将近、几乎）正是修\n饰数字、表示接近某量的标准副词。\n【竞争词】F) increasingly 义为\"越来越\"，不修饰固定数字\nincreasingly 表\"日益、越来越\"，描述渐变趋势，不能修饰 40 percent 这样的固定百分比；它\n是第 30 空 ___ becoming 的正解。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) reducing\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 from 后接动名词\n【依据】\"减少需求\"用 reducing，且现成-ing 形态\nsave millions of liters of water a year simply from __(34)__ the demands of cotton\nproduction——靠\"减少\"棉花生产的需求来省水，reducing the demands 是地道及物搭\n配，且词库直接给的就是-ing 形式，无需变形。\n【竞争词】M) shrink 形态需变且与 demands 搭配弱\nshrink 是原形，作动名词得改成 shrinking 才合 from 后位置，而词库取词须逐字、本无-ing\n形；且 shrink 多指物体/数量自身收缩，\"shrink the demands\"不如\"reduce the demands\"地\n道。shrink 正是第 35 空 as water supplies ___ 的答案，留给那里。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— shrink 也表\"减少\"、看着也通。第 34 空 from __(34)__\nthe demands：shrink 词义也是\"减少\"，易抢 reducing 的位置；防坑是\"reduce the\ndemands\"才是地道及物搭配，且 shrink 留给第 35 空。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abstracts", "B) abundant", "C) awareness", "D) conscience", "E) exact", "F) increasingly", "G) intense", "H) mend", "I) nearly", "J) reckoning", "K) reducing", "L) sew", "M) shrink", "N) statistics", "O) textile"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) shrink\n动词\n【词性槽】as 从句主语 supplies 后缺谓语\n【依据】水供应\"减少\"，shrink 表自身缩减最贴\nas water supplies __(35)__, farmers and consumers need to be more conscious 说\"随\n着水供应减少\"，shrink（缩小、减少）正描述供应量自身收缩，与全文\"清洁水日益稀\n缺\"呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to Francis Dittoh, their speech-based weather information service was meant to be inexpensive and easy to use.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】便宜易用的天气服务\n【定位】The service was designed to be cheap and easy to run, says Mr Dittoh.\n这项服务被设计得便宜又易于运行，Dittoh 先生说。\n【改写】inexpensive and easy to use ↔ cheap and easy to run\n便宜易用同义替换\ntheir speech-based weather information service ↔\nMr Meteo takes the online weather forecast, converts it to a short recording\n服务名与其描述对应\n【辨邻】 D D 段借 Dittoh 之口讲农民 underpaid for their produce 的困境，并未提服务\"便宜易\n用\"，不是本句源头。\nsays Francis Dittoh, a researcher behind Mr Meteo, a speech-based weather information service", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Using voice instead of typing enables doctors to spend more time taking care of patients.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】语音让医生多陪病人\n【定位】This enables them to spend less time on administration and more time with patients.\n这使他们能少花时间在行政事务上、多花时间陪病人。\n【改写】Using voice instead of typing ↔\ndictate 150 words a minute, three times faster than typing on a keyboard\n口述即用语音代打字\nspend more time taking care of patients ↔ more time with patients\n陪病人即照料病人\n【辨邻】 L L 段讲诊所装系统、助手 automatically enters the information into the right\nfields，落脚在效率与减少延误，没提\"多花时间陪病人\"，故非本句源头。\na smart assistant automatically enters the information into the right fields on a web form", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is extremely difficult to convert voice into text because of different pronunciations.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】因发音不同语音转文字难\n【定位】Even turning your voice into text—automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest\nproblems to solve, as there are as many ways to pronounce things as there are people on\nthe planet.\n甚至把你的语音转成文字——也就是自动语音识别——都是最难解决的问题之一，因为发音\n方式和地球上的人一样多。\n【改写】convert voice into text ↔ turning your voice into text\n转文字同义替换\nextremely difficult ↔ one of the hardest problems to solve\n极难即最难之一\ndifferent pronunciations ↔ as many ways to pronounce things as there are people on the planet\n比喻还原为发音各异", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "African farmers unable to read often don't have access to important information conveyed online.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不识字非洲农民失信息\n【定位】What use is written online content if you can't read? That is the situation facing illiterate\nAfrican farmers. They are often denied crucial information the web offers many others.\n如果你不会阅读，网上的文字内容有什么用？这正是不识字的非洲农民面临的处境。他们常\n常被剥夺了网络提供给许多其他人的关键信息。\n【改写】African farmers unable to read ↔ illiterate African farmers\n不识字与不会读同义\ndon't have access to important information conveyed online ↔\ndenied crucial information the web offers many others\n被剥夺即无法获取\n【辨邻】 E E 段讲农民抱怨 rainfall predictions 不准、祖辈方法失灵，焦点是天气，没讲\"因不识\n字而拿不到网络信息\"，故非本句源头。\nThe most frequently heard complaint is about rainfall predictions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Some phone users worry advertisers will take advantage of voice assistants to send ads directly to them.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】担心广告商利用语音助手\n【定位】They worry assistants will one day be used to deliver advertising directly to us.\n他们担心助手有一天会被用来直接向我们投放广告。\n【改写】send ads directly to them ↔ deliver advertising directly to us\n投放广告同义替换\nSome phone users worry ↔ They worry\n担心主语对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The spoken web is helpful when one's hands are occupied.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】双手被占用时有帮助\n【定位】Using voice also makes sense when you're doing other things with your hands.\n当你正用双手做别的事情时，使用语音也很合理。\n【改写】one's hands are occupied ↔ doing other things with your hands\n手被占用即手在忙别的\nThe spoken web is helpful ↔ Using voice also makes sense\n有帮助即合理\n【辨邻】 K K 段讲 depend on the context 并举医生口述提速的好处，没讲\"双手被占用\"这个场\n景，故非本句源头。\nThe benefits of using voice instead of tapping fingers obviously depend on the context", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Some people believe online interaction would soon depend mainly on voice.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】上网将主要靠语音\n【定位】Some think voice could soon take over from typing and clicking as the main way to\ninteract online.\n有些人认为语音可能很快取代打字和点击，成为网上互动的主要方式。\n【改写】online interaction would soon depend mainly on voice ↔\nvoice could soon take over from typing and clicking as the main way to interact online\n取代打字即主要靠语音\nSome people believe ↔ Some think\n认为同义对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Setting up a spoken web is by no means an easy task.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】建语音网络绝非易事\n【定位】But building the spoken web—web-to-voice and voice-to-web—isn't straightforward.\n但构建语音网络——网页转语音、语音转网页——并不简单。\n【改写】Setting up a spoken web ↔ building the spoken web\n建语音网络同义\nby no means an easy task ↔ isn't straightforward\n绝非易事即并不简单\n【辨邻】 H H 段讲 automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest problems，是\"建语\n音网络\"之下的一个具体子难题，并非\"建语音网络绝非易事\"这个总判断的出处。\nEven turning your voice into text—automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest problems\nto solve", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Weather information is extremely important to farmers.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】天气信息对农民极重要\n【定位】Yet knowing when it's going to rain is vital for farmers wanting to sow seeds, irrigate crops or\ntake their animals out to the fields to feed on grass.\n然而，对于想要播种、灌溉庄稼或把牲畜赶到地里吃草的农民来说，知道何时下雨至关\n重要。\n【改写】Weather information ↔ knowing when it's going to rain\n天气信息含何时下雨\nextremely important to farmers ↔ vital for farmers\n极重要即至关重要\n【辨邻】 D D 段讲农民因不了解 the prevailing prices 而被压价，焦点是价格信息，没讲\"天气信\n息对农民极重要\"，故非本句源头。\nunderpaid for their produce because they might be unaware of the prevailing prices\nF F 段讲 Mr Meteo takes the online weather forecast, converts it to a short\nrecording 这个服务的运作，没讲\"天气信息对农民极重要\"这个判断，故非本句源头。\nMr Meteo takes the online weather forecast, converts it to a short recording in the\nappropriate language", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some people are concerned about privacy because their phones are constantly collecting their personal information.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】因手机持续收数据担心隐私\n【定位】Our phones are always near us and they are collecting data about us all the time. This has\nalready raised privacy concerns.\n我们的手机总在身边，时刻都在收集关于我们的数据。这已经引发了隐私方面的担忧。\n【改写】their phones are constantly collecting their personal information ↔\nthey are collecting data about us all the time\n持续收数据同义\nconcerned about privacy ↔ raised privacy concerns\n担心隐私即引发隐私担忧", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What are big houses promoted to be in the United States?", "options": ["A) A luxury for the homeless.", "B) A reward for industriousness.", "C) An abundant source of comforts.", "D) An absolute necessity for happiness."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Q46 问的是 What are big houses promoted to be，要的是文中明写的一个事实陈述，不\n需推理。\n【拆句】Q46 是一个被动语态疑问句，主干为\"big houses are promoted to be 什么\"，promoted\nto be 后面接的内容就是答案要补的成分，结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】Big houses are advertised as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a\nbasic necessity into a luxury.\n大房子被宣传成对努力工作和勤勉的一种回报，把住房从基本必需品变成了奢侈品。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文宣传是面向勤奋者的回报，A luxury for the homeless 把奢侈品安到 homeless 头\n上，而原文 homelessness 只是危机背景，并非大房子的宣传对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\na reward for hard work and diligence ↔ A reward for industriousness\n预判的\"对勤奋的回报\"在 A reward for industriousness 中 reward 与 industriousness\n双双对应，锁定该项。\nC) 答非所问\nAn abundant source of comforts 取自第三段及背景里 amenities/abound 的语义，\n但 Q46 问的是大房子被\"宣传成什么\"，定位句给的是 reward，不是\"舒适之源\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说大房子使住房 from a basic necessity into a luxury（从必需变奢侈），An\nabsolute necessity for happiness 反把它说成\"幸福的绝对必需品\"，与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is one of the consequences of living big?", "options": ["A) Many Americans' quality of life has become lower.", "B) People from diverse backgrounds no longer socialize.", "C) People no longer have access to public swimming pools.", "D) Many Americans' private life has been negatively affected."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the consequences 用 one of 限定，答案是原文列举的多项后果中的一\n项，属可直接定位的细节。\n【拆句】Q47 主干为\"What is one of the consequences\"，of living big 是后置定语修饰\nconsequences，one of 提示原文有多项后果，结构简单。\n【定位】Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together,\nhave increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities\navailable to all, and a reduced quality of life for many.\n第二，曾经的公共空间——不同背景的人过去常在此相聚——日益被私有化，导致面向所有\n人的公共设施数量减少，许多人的生活质量随之下降。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na reduced quality of life for many ↔ Many Americans' quality of life has become lower\n预判的\"生活质量下降\"与 quality of life has become lower 字面同义，直接锁定。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说公共空间被私有化、不同背景的人\"过去\"常相聚（used to），并未说人们 no\nlonger socialize（彻底不再社交），属过度引申。\nC) 以偏概全\n游泳池只是说明私有化的一个例子，原文说 public municipal pools were often\nclosed（常被关闭），no longer have access to public swimming pools 把\"常被\n关\"绝对成\"再也用不上\"，且以个例顶替本题要的总后果。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文受影响的是 quality of life（生活质量），private life has been negatively\naffected 偷换成 private life（私人生活），概念不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What questions arise from living big?", "options": ["A) Questions related to moral principles.", "B) Questions having to do with labor cost.", "C) Questions about what lifestyle to promote.", "D) Questions concerning housing development."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What questions arise from living big 问的是原文明确点出的问题性质，文中有直接对应\n的形容词限定，属细节。\n【拆句】Q48 主干\"What questions arise\"，from living big 为原因状语，整句问\"住大房子引发哪\n类问题\"，无复杂从句。\n【定位】The trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions.\n因此，住房越来越大的趋势引出了伦理（道德）层面的问题。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nethical questions ↔ Questions related to moral principles\n预判的\"伦理/道德问题\"与 moral principles 同义，直接锁定。\nB) 答非所问\nlabor cost 取自第四段反问里 using the low-wage labor of others，但那只是伦理问\n题的内容举例，问题的\"性质\"是伦理性，labor cost 答非所问。\nC) 张冠李戴\nlifestyle to promote 接近第五段\"该提倡什么生活方式\"的话题，但 Q48 问的是第四段\n引发的问题性质，what lifestyle to promote 张冠李戴到了下一段内容。\nD) 无中生有\nhousing development（住房开发/发展）在第四段问题里并无对应表述，原文谈的是\n伦理而非开发问题，属凭空捏造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What kind of social system does the author think is unacceptable?", "options": ["A) One in which the wealthy exploit the low-wage laborers building their houses.", "B) One in which the rich purchase amenities at an increasingly unjustifiable price.", "C) One in which the upper classes deprive the lower classes of affordable housing.", "D) One in which the affluent enjoy a more comfortable life at the expense of the poor."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What kind of social system does the author think is unacceptable 答案在原文有 a\nsystem in which... 的直接描述，是可定位的细节，而非需推断的整体态度。\n【拆句】Q49 主干\"What kind of social system is unacceptable\"，中间插入 does the author\nthink 表明这是作者的判断，从句嵌套但语义清晰：找作者认为不可接受的那种制度。\n【定位】Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent\nmeans a reduction in amenities for the poor?\n我们是否愿意接受这样一种制度：富人购买的便利设施增加，就意味着穷人可享的便利设施\n减少？\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文第一个反问确提到 using the low-wage labor of others，但 the low-wage\nlaborers building their houses 把\"他人的低薪劳动\"窄化为\"盖房工人\"，且第二个反问\n（富人便利↔穷人便利）才是更完整的对应，A 项以偏概全。\nB) 无中生有\n原文说富人购买便利使穷人便利减少，从未谈 an increasingly unjustifiable price（越\n来越不合理的价格），价格维度是捏造的。\nC) 偷换概念\ndeprive the lower classes of affordable housing（剥夺下层的平价住房）取自首段背\n景 Affordable housing is inadequate，但作者第四段否定的制度是\"便利的增减对\n立\"，不是剥夺平价住房，概念被偷换。\nD) ✓ 正确\nan increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor\n↔ the affluent enjoy a more comfortable life at the expense of the poor\n预判的\"富人享乐、穷人受损\"恰是 enjoy a more comfortable life at the expense of\nthe poor 的含义，锁定该项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author advocate for people to live well?", "options": ["A) Finding ways to turn private spaces into public ones.", "B) Building more houses affordable to those less affluent.", "C) More public spaces created for everyone to enjoy.", "D) All amenities made accessible to the rich and the poor alike."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advocate 答案是末段作者直接给出的建议，有明写对应句，属细\n节定位题。\n【拆句】Q50 主干\"What does the author advocate\"，for people to live well 是目的状语（为了\n让人们住得好），整句问\"作者为此提倡什么\"，结构清楚。\n【定位】A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible\nspaces and amenities for all.\n比起为少数人盖更大的房子，更好的目标是为所有人创造更多可公共使用的空间与设施。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n作者主张是\"创造更多公共空间\"，并未说要把私人空间 turn private spaces into public\nones（改造成公共空间），这一具体做法原文没有。\nB) 与原文相反\nBuilding more houses affordable 回到了\"盖房子\"，而作者主张的恰恰是别再盖大\n房子、转向公共空间，且 affordable housing 是首段背景非作者的提倡，方向与主张\n相悖。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncreate more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all ↔\nMore public spaces created for everyone to enjoy\n预判的\"为人人创造公共空间\"与 More public spaces created for everyone to enjoy\n字面同义，锁定该项。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是\"为所有人创造公共空间与设施\"，All amenities made accessible to the rich\nand the poor alike 用 All amenities 把范围绝对化为\"所有便利设施都对贫富开放\"，超\n出作者所说的\"公共空间\"范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think of most entrepreneurs?", "options": ["A) They are more willing to risk their own lives.", "B) They are more ambitious than ordinary people.", "C) They achieve greater nonconventional success.", "D) They have more positive qualities than most of us."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think of most entrepreneurs? 直接问对一个明确对象的看\n法，答案能在原文某句找到对应陈述，不需跨段综合，属细节题而非推断。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author think of most entrepreneurs? 是 think of 引导的特殊疑问\n句，think of sb 意为\"对某人的看法\"，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-\naverage ambition.\n我们创业圈里的大多数人，都被赋予——或者说被困于——高于常人的野心。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说野心者 willing to take more risks 指愿冒事业风险，选项却说 risk their own\nlives（拿命冒险），把\"风险\"偷换成\"生命危险\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nhigher-than-average ambition ↔ more ambitious than ordinary people\n选项 They are more ambitious than ordinary people 与预判\"野心高于普通人\"完全吻\n合，是定位句的同义替换。\nC) 无中生有\n原文谈的是 conventional \"success\"（传统成功），选项却说 nonconventional\nsuccess（非传统成功），文中从无此说法。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文只说野心 this is a positive quality 是\"一种\"积极特质，选项却说创业者 more\npositive qualities than most of us\"比我们多\"，既夸大数量又把比较对象搞错（原文\nmost of us 本就包含创业者）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author imply by saying \"this isn't always the case\" (Line 1, Para. 3)?", "options": ["A) Ambitious people may not have a greater chance of success.", "B) Ambitious people may not have more positive qualities.", "C) Entrepreneurs' ambition does as much good as harm.", "D) Entrepreneurs are more naturally driven to success."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author imply by saying \"this isn't always the case\" 用 imply by\nsaying 问一个具体句子的含义，要求理解 this 指代什么、否定的是什么，属词义句意题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author imply，by saying \"...\" 是方式状语；关键在引文里的\nthis 是指代词，须回原文找它替代的上文内容；isn't always 是部分否定，意为\"并非总\n是\"，不是全盘否定。\n【定位】Actually, this isn't always the case.\n实际上，情况并非总是如此。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nyou are more likely to succeed ↔ may not have a greater chance of success\n选项 Ambitious people may not have a greater chance of success 正是\"this（野心\n者更易成功）＋部分否定\"的还原，与预判一致。\nB) 偷换概念\nthis 指代的是\"更易成功\"，并非\"积极特质\"；选项 may not have more positive\nqualities 把否定矛头指向上一段的 positive quality，找错了 this 的指代。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文只说极端野心 may end up doing more harm than good（弊\"大于\"利），选项\n却说 does as much good as harm（利害\"相当\"），把\"大于\"篡改成\"相等\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 more naturally driven to success 几乎照搬上文 more naturally driven to set\ngoals，可这恰是本句要否定的观点，方向正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say is of extreme importance for one to become a successful entrepreneur?", "options": ["A) Holding on to one's original vision.", "B) Being able to adapt to new situations.", "C) Focusing determinedly on one particular goal.", "D) Avoiding radical change in one's career direction."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say is of extreme importance 用 say 直问作者明说的内容，\n答案是原文一句强调重要性的话，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say is of extreme importance for one to become a\nsuccessful entrepreneur?，主干是 What...is of extreme importance；of+抽象名词等于\n形容词，of extreme importance＝extremely important；for one to become... 是不定\n式目的状语，指明\"为了成为成功创业者\"。\n【定位】This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is\nvital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur.\n这是有问题的，因为它会损害你适应新环境的能力，而这种能力对于想成为成功创业者的人\n至关重要。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说过度野心导致 focus too determinedly on one particular vision 反而有害，需\nstraying from your original vision；选项 Holding on to one's original vision（死守\n原愿景）正是文章要批评的做法。\nB) ✓ 正确\nadapt to new circumstances ↔ adapt to new situations\n选项 Being able to adapt to new situations 与预判\"适应新环境的能力\"几乎逐词对\n应，确为答案。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Focusing determinedly on one particular goal 直接抄自 focus too\ndeterminedly on one particular vision，而原文说这是过度野心的\"副作用\"，绝非\"极\n其重要\"之事。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说有时须 change direction\"改变方向\"，选项却说 Avoiding radical change in\none's career direction\"避免大改方向\"，与文意相左。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How do the most ambitious entrepreneurs regard failure in their endeavor?", "options": ["A) It will awaken them to reality.", "B) It is a lesson they have to learn.", "C) It means the end of their career.", "D) It will result in a slow recovery."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do the most ambitious entrepreneurs regard failure 问一个明确群体对失败\n的态度，原文有明文表述，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 How do the most ambitious entrepreneurs regard failure in their endeavor?，\nregard sth 意为\"看待某事\"，in their endeavor（在其奋斗中）是范围状语；结构清晰、无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and\nimpossible to recover from.\n不幸的是，对那些野心最大的创业者来说，一次失败被视为灾难性的、且无法翻身的。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nawaken them to reality 对应的是第六段 failure is viewed as something closer to\nreality，那讲的是\"野心有限者\"，而非题干问的\"野心最大者\"。\nB) 张冠李戴\na lesson they have to learn 对应第六段 every failure you survive is a learning\nexperience，同样是说\"野心有限者\"，张冠李戴。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndisastrous, and impossible to recover from ↔ means the end of their career\n选项 It means the end of their career 把\"灾难、无法恢复\"提炼成\"事业到头\"，与预判\n一致，确为答案。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说 impossible to recover from\"无法恢复\"，选项却说 result in a slow\nrecovery\"导致缓慢恢复\"，恢复从\"不可能\"被偷改成\"慢\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author advise us to do concerning ambition?", "options": ["A) Distinguish between conventional success and our life goal.", "B) Follow the example of the most ambitious entrepreneurs.", "C) Avoid taking unnecessary risks when starting a business.", "D) Prioritize health and happiness over material success."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author advise us to do concerning ambition? 问作者的建议，原文\n未必有一句直说\"我建议你……\"，需综合全文对野心利弊的论述提炼，属推断题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author advise us to do concerning ambition?，advise sb to do\n意为\"建议某人做某事\"，concerning ambition（关于野心）是话题状语；结构平铺直叙、\n无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文要区分的是 conventional \"success\" 与 health and happiness，选项 Distinguish\nbetween conventional success and our life goal 把后一项换成了文中没有的\"人生目\n标\"，区分对象被改错。\nB) 与原文相反\n全文都在指出 the most ambitious entrepreneurs 看待失败的方式有害，选项 Follow\nthe example of the most ambitious entrepreneurs 却劝人效仿他们，与作者立场正\n相反。\nC) 以偏概全\nAvoid taking unnecessary risks 只关乎 take more risks 这一点，并非全文劝诫的落\n脚；作者最终落点是健康幸福，而非\"少冒险\"，以偏概全且无中生有。\nD) ✓ 正确\nsuch success means nothing for their health and happiness—and if you don't have health and\nhappiness, what else could possibly matter?\n↔ Prioritize health and happiness over material success\n选项 Prioritize health and happiness over material success 与主线\"健康幸福重于成\n功\"吻合，且统摄全文对过度野心的批评，是建议题应有的全局答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
